________________________________________
2. 📌 Problem Understanding
What is a Line Editor?
A line editor is a simple text editor that works with text one line at a time through the terminal.
What are we building?
We are building a command-line Line Editor in C that allows the user to:
•	Add new lines
•	Insert lines
•	View the document
•	Delete lines
•	Replace lines
•	Find and replace text
•	Get help about commands
•	Exit the program
The document is stored temporarily in memory while the program is running.
________________________________________
3. 🔄 Program Flow
The program follows this basic flow:
User enters command
        ↓
Program interprets command
        ↓
Identifies line number / text
        ↓
Performs the operation
        ↓
Document is updated
        ↓
User can display the document
________________________________________
4. 🗂️ Data Structure Choice
Dynamic Array of Strings
We use a dynamic array of strings to store the lines of the document.
Example:
lines
  ↓
[0] → "Hello World"
[1] → "Welcome to C"
[2] → "Line Editor"
Each position stores one line of text.
________________________________________
5. 💡 Why We Chose It
We chose a dynamic array because:
•	📈 Its size can grow when more lines are added.
•	⚡ Lines can be accessed directly using their position.
•	💾 It works well with malloc() and realloc().
•	🧩 It is simple to implement in C.
•	📄 It is suitable for a small text document.
Trade-off
When inserting or deleting a line, other lines may need to be shifted. For a small document, this is acceptable.
________________________________________
6. 📊 Data Maintained
The program maintains three main values:
Data	Purpose
lines	Stores the text lines
count	Number of lines currently stored
capacity	Number of line slots currently allocated
Example:
capacity = 5
count    = 3

[Line 1] [Line 2] [Line 3] [Empty] [Empty]
________________________________________
7. ⌨️ Command Set
Command	Purpose
append	Add a line at the end
insert	Add a line at a specific position
print	Display all lines
delete	Delete a specific line
replace	Replace an existing line
findreplace	Find and replace text
help	Display available commands
quit	Exit the editor
________________________________________
8. 💾 Memory Management
The program uses dynamic memory to store the document.
•	malloc() → Allocates memory.
•	realloc() → Increases the array size when required.
•	free() → Releases memory that is no longer needed.
Before exiting, the program releases the allocated memory to avoid memory leaks.
________________________________________
9. 🛡️ Error Handling
The program checks for common errors such as:
•	❌ Invalid commands
•	❌ Invalid line numbers
•	❌ Empty document
•	❌ Invalid input
•	❌ Attempting to access a line that does not exist
•	❌ Memory allocation failure
The program should handle these cases safely without crashing.
________________________________________
10. 🧪 Testing Plan
We will test the following cases:
Normal Cases
•	Add multiple lines
•	Display the document
•	Insert a line
•	Delete a line
•	Replace a line
•	Find and replace text
Edge Cases
•	Empty document
•	Insert at the beginning
•	Insert at the end
•	Delete the only line
•	Invalid line number
•	Non-existing line
•	Invalid command
•	Large input
Memory Testing
•	Add many lines
•	Grow the dynamic array
•	Delete lines
•	Exit after creating lines
•	Check that allocated memory is properly freed
________________________________________
11. 🎯 Expected Result
The final program should provide a simple, reliable command-line text editor that allows users to modify a small document using commands while safely managing memory.
The program should also handle invalid input and edge cases without crashing.
________________________________________
📌 Conclusion
The project uses a dynamic array of strings because it provides a simple and flexible way to store and modify lines of text. The design focuses on easy line access, dynamic memory management, basic editing operations, and safe error handling.

